<interrogationes unitas="insula">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I change a value in the insula state tree?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_mutare mutare_ramum insula_attributum_ponere InsulaMutator">Write
      an InsulaMutator and pass it to insula_mutare (whole tree) or
      mutare_ramum (one branch); inside it call
      insula_attributum_ponere(nodus, piscina, intern, titulus, valor)
      with the piscina and intern the mutator was given. The mutator
      runs on a DUPLICATE (the live tree written to text and parsed
      into the other piscina); owners and canons judge it, and only
      then does it replace the live tree, so a write is all or
      nothing. Use insula_attributum_ponere rather than
      stml_attributum_addere: the latter always appends, and
      stml_attributum_capere would keep returning the old
      value.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I change the tree through the pointer insula_radix gives me?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_radix insula_restituere insula_mendacium insula_causa">No.
      insula_radix and insula_ramus_nodus are for reading only. The
      gate keeps the text of the last honest write;
      insula_restituere serializes the live tree, compares, and on a
      mismatch sets insula_mendacium, puts "mendacium: insula extra
      portam mutata" in insula_causa and rebuilds the tree from the
      honest text, discarding your change. mendacium is decided anew by
      each restituere, not sticky. Only restituere checks: the next
      gate write copies the live tree as it stands, so an out-of-gate
      change that reaches it first is carried into the new honest
      text unjudged.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep a StmlNodus pointer from the insula across writes?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_radix insula_mutare insula_restituere insula_attributum">Not
      safely. The tree lives in one of two alternating piscinas, and
      every write attempt (even a refused one) and every
      insula_restituere starts by emptying the piscina that is NOT
      live. A refused write leaves your pointer valid; an accepted
      write or a restituere moves the live tree to the other piscina,
      so your pointer then reads the stale old copy, and the next
      attempt or restituere empties it. Look nodes up again after
      each write. Attribute values are different: the chorda* from
      insula_attributum points into the interner and outlives the
      swap.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find out why a write was refused?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_causa mutare_ramum insula_mutare">Read
      insula_causa(repo) right after a FALSUM return. It names the
      reason: an owner refusal ("dominus: X possidetur a ..."), a canon
      refusal with the canon's first vitium, "ramus abest" for a
      missing branch, or "mutatio extra ramum" when a branch mutator
      touched nodes outside its subtree. A successful write does NOT
      clear it, so a non-empty causa alone does not mean the last
      write failed.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does every insula write serialize and re-parse the whole tree?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_mutare insula_restituere" via="lib/insula.worklog.md">The
      round trip through text IS the rehydration discipline: a tree
      that cannot survive being written and read back cannot be
      written at all, and the mutator working on a copy is what makes
      a refused write leave the live tree untouched. The worklog
      measured the cost (S8d, S8e): at size the time goes to owner and
      canon judging and to persistence, not to the copy.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I give an app or widget its own part of the tree?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_ramus insula_ramus_liberi insula_ramum_initiare mutare_ramum insula_ramus_ex_via">A
      branch: insula_ramus(repo, elementum, id) names the root's child
      &lt;elementum id="id"/&gt;, and insula_ramus_liberi(&amp;parens, e,
      id) a child of another branch, at any depth. The path
      ("a:1/b:2") is interned, so a branch is a cheap value to copy.
      insula_ramum_initiare(&amp;ramus, stml) adds the element under its
      parent if it is absent (the parent must exist), and mutare_ramum
      hands the mutator the branch's node in the duplicate and refuses
      changes outside it. insula_ramus_ex_via goes back from a path
      string to a branch.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can insula_ramum_initiare create the branch from any element I pass?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_ramum_initiare insula_ramus_nodus">Only
      if the element's name and id match the branch. The function
      appends whatever element the STML holds and does not check it
      against the branch's elementum and id, so
      &lt;b id="2"/&gt; for branch "a:1" returns VERUM while
      insula_ramus_nodus stays NIHIL, and each later call appends
      another &lt;b/&gt;. A malformed STML string also commits an
      empty write (versio goes up) before returning FALSUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make an attribute writable only by one action or system?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_dominum_ponere insula_ramus_dominum_ponere insula_dominos_legere insula_scriptorem_ponere">Register
      an owner with insula_dominum_ponere (root) or
      insula_ramus_dominum_ponere (branch), or read a
      &lt;domini&gt;&lt;dominus attributum scriptor systemata/&gt;&lt;/domini&gt;
      table with insula_dominos_legere. A write that adds, changes or
      removes that attribute is refused unless the current scriptor
      (insula_scriptorem_ponere, set around the call; empty =
      anonymous) is the owner or one of the systemata. The usual idiom
      saves repo-&gt;scriptor, sets it, writes, and restores it. Register
      owners AFTER insula_ramum_initiare, or the anonymous initial
      write is refused.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I validate the tree's shape on every write?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_ponere_canonem insula_ramus_canonem_ponere canon_iudicare">insula_ponere_canonem
      sets (and replaces) the root canon; insula_ramus_canonem_ponere
      gives a branch its own canon. Each canon judges its node minus
      descendant branches that have their own canon, and a write is
      judged by the nearest canon above its path plus the canons below
      it. Set a branch canon BEFORE initiating the branch. Note that
      insula_ramus_canonem_ponere appends: calling it again for the
      same branch does not replace the first canon, which keeps
      winning for writes through that branch, while a root write is
      judged by both.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I set an attribute to the empty string?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_attributum_ponere insula_attributum_tollere chorda_internare_ex_literis stml_scribere">No.
      Values are interned and chorda_internare_ex_literis returns NIHIL
      for "", so the attribute is stored with a NIHIL value and
      insula_attributum reads NIHIL, as if absent. Worse, the STML
      writer emits a NIHIL value as a bare attribute name, which the
      parser reads as "true", so after the next round trip (the next
      gate write or insula_restituere) the attribute reads "true".
      Remove it with insula_attributum_tollere instead (swap-remove;
      attribute order is not meaningful).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I persist or log every change to the tree?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_ponere_actarium InsulaActarius insula_scribere insula_versio">Register
      an InsulaActarius with insula_ponere_actarium: it is called after
      every successful write with the new root and the written path
      (empty = root, so anything may have changed). Nothing outside
      that path changed, so it may skip other parts.
      insula_scribere(repo, piscina) gives the whole tree as pretty
      STML in your piscina, and insula_versio counts accepted writes.
      An accepted write counts even if it changed nothing:
      InsulaMutator returns vacuum and cannot cancel, so decide before
      calling the gate.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I free an InsulaRepositorium when I am done with it?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_repositorium_creare piscina_generare_dynamicum">No.
      insula_repositorium_creare makes two private dynamic piscinas
      (insula_a and insula_b, XVI * M bytes initially) for the
      alternating trees, and there is no function that destroys them.
      It also returns NIHIL for malformed source STML, so the reason
      it set in causa cannot be read.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
